Learning GitHub Actions 

📌 What are Jobs in GitHub Actions?
A job is a set of steps that run on the same runner to perform a specific task in a workflow.
A workflow can contain multiple jobs, such as building an application, running tests, and deploying it. By default, independent jobs can run in parallel, while job dependencies can be configured using needs.

🛠️ Practical Example:

name: Jobs Demo  

on:   
    push:
           branches:
                  - main  

  jobs: 
        build:
             runs-on: ubuntu-latest 
               steps:       
                    - name: Build Application
                      run: echo "Building application..."    

  test:     
     runs-on: ubuntu-latest     
     needs: build     
     steps:  
              - name: Run Tests         
                run: echo "Running tests..."

🔍 How it works:
jobs: Defines the jobs in the workflow.
build: Executes the build task.
test: Runs only after the build job succeeds because of needs: build.
runs-on: Specifies the runner environment.

💡 Key Takeaway:
Jobs help organize CI/CD workflows into separate tasks, making automation easier to manage and helping define the execution order.
